Mojo runtime libraries (redistributed in every macOS binary artifact)
    Path        licenses/LICENSE.mojo-runtime.txt
    Upstream    https://github.com/modular/modular
    License     Apache License 2.0 with LLVM Exceptions
    Copyright   Modular Inc

    Every macOS artifact — the `m0serve` bundle and the PyPI wheel —
    contains three Mojo runtime libraries, because the artifact resolves
    them at load time and is useless without them:

        libKGENCompilerRTShared.dylib
        libMSupportGlobals.dylib
        libAsyncRTRuntimeGlobals.dylib

    MODIFIED, minimally and mechanically: each library's Mach-O install name
    and rpath are rewritten so the set resolves beside itself rather than in
    the directory it was built in, and each is re-signed ad-hoc because arm64
    invalidates a signature on any Mach-O edit. No source was changed and no
    code was recompiled. `licenses/NOTICE.m0serve.txt` travels inside the
    bundle and the wheel and states this, which is Apache section 4(b).

    The two binaries differ on Linux, and the difference is Modular's
    linking strategy rather than ours. The libm0core SHARED LIBRARY is
    statically linked there: no DT_NEEDED, no Modular file needed beside
    it, the runtime code embedded in the library. The m0serve EXECUTABLE is
    not: it links the same three libraries dynamically on Linux exactly as
    on macOS, so its bundle and its wheel carry them as files on both
    platforms, with DT_RUNPATH rewritten to $ORIGIN.

    (This entry said Linux artifacts were statically linked without
    qualification until 2026-08-25. That was measured on libm0core.so and
    assumed of the executable; the first Linux CI run to bundle m0serve
    showed it needs all three. Either way the runtime code is redistributed
    and this entry covers it — a licence outcome should not turn on which
    platform links dynamically.)

    ON THE LICENCE, because the evidence is not unanimous. Mojo's compiler
    and tooling were open sourced on 2026-08-18 under Apache 2.0 with LLVM
    Exceptions; modular/modular's root LICENSE says exactly that with no
    carve-out, and the runtime sources (KGEN/, AsyncRT/, Support/lib/) are in
    that repository. Against that, the wheel shipping the prebuilt binaries
    — mojo_compiler, checked again at 1.1.0.dev2026082305, five days after
    the relicensing — still declares
    `License: LicenseRef-MAX-Platform-Software-License`, and ships no licence
    file of its own. That wheel contains only modular/bin, modular/lib and
    mojo: the compiler and its runtime, no MAX components, which is exactly
    the scope that was open sourced.

    Proceeding on the basis that these binaries are builds of the
    Apache-licensed sources, and complying with Apache section 4 IN FULL —
    licence text above, attribution and modification notice in the bundle —
    rather than relying on the LLVM Exception to excuse sections 4(a), 4(b)
    and 4(d). That exception covers portions embedded into Object form by
    compiling one's own source, which describes libm0core itself and the
    statically linked Linux artifact; it does not obviously reach shipping
    runtime libraries as separate files, so nothing here depends on it
    doing so.

