      - http/response.mojo, event_loop.mojo, server.mojo (2026-09-23): a
        1xx, 204 or 304 carries no invented entity headers, and a 1xx or
        204 no Content-Length at all (SPEC A21; RFC 9110 §8.6). The two
        server-side HTTPResponse constructors add their `Content-Type:
        application/octet-stream` and `Content-Length` defaults only for
        a status that has content, and neither when passed
        `invent_entity_headers=False`, which the gateway uses to relay an
        application's head as sent (SPEC K12). `is_bodiless_status` and
        `enforce_bodiless_framing` are new beside them. The event loop and
        the blocking server apply the rule to every such response before
        its head is encoded: a 1xx's or 204's own length and any body are
        dropped, a file body's descriptor closed, and a 304 keeps its
        length. And `_finish_response` answers a HEAD whose handler opened
        a stream (a hold, a native SSE route) with its head alone: the
        stream flag is cleared, the length popped, and the subscription the
        handler made dropped through `sse_slot_disconnected` (SPEC L27).
        The rule drops a handler's own `Transfer-Encoding` from a 1xx or
        204 too (RFC 9112 §6.1), a 304 keeping it as it keeps a length, and
        the loop's stream branch asks `is_bodiless_status` rather than
        spelling the three statuses itself.

