        closes (`_answers_the_last_request`; SPEC A30). The flag closed the
        connection as that answer landed (review record LF43).

      - header.mojo (2026-10-08): `host_value_is_valid` holds a `Host`
        field value, and an absolute-form target's authority, to
        `uri-host [ ":" port ]` (RFC 9110 §7.2, RFC 3986 §3.2.2-3.2.3),
        and `parse_request_headers` refuses one that is not, 400 (SPEC
        B27). It served `Host: a b` and `Host: u@a` (review record LF64).

      - header.mojo (2026-10-08): `parse_request_headers` reads the
        `Transfer-Encoding` list as bytes, split on its commas, each member
        compared with `chunked` under ASCII case folding, and
        `ParsedRequestHeaders.is_chunked_body` asks the final member the
        same way. Both used `String.lower()`, which folds by Unicode's
        rules (KELVIN SIGN to `k`) and reads the value as UTF-8 (review
        record LF58).

      - http/request.mojo, loop/state.mojo, loop/request.mojo
        (2026-10-08): `HTTPRequest.from_parsed` takes the server's host and
        port, which `LoopState` splits from its address once
        (`split_server_address`), and parses the target alone, so
        `uri.host` and `uri.port` are the same for every target shape (SPEC
        A37). The fast path put the whole address in
        `host`, and the full parse read the address prefixed to the target
        (review record LF54).

      - http/chunked.mojo (2026-10-08): `HTTPChunkedDecoder.decode` has an
        `IN_CHUNK_SIZE_END` state between the size and its extension: it
        takes whitespace, then `;` or the CR, and refuses anything else;
        `IN_CHUNK_EXT` refuses a control byte other than HTAB, or DEL
        (SPEC B28). The extension state took every byte to the CR, so `5 5`
        was a chunk of 5 (review record LF60).

      - http/request.mojo (2026-10-08): `HTTPRequest.encode` and
        `write_to` write `uri.request_uri` (`_target`), where they wrote the
        percent-decoded `uri.path` and its query; `from_parsed` keeps the
        target as it arrived in `request_uri`, `*` included (SPEC A38,
        review record LF62).

      - header.mojo, http/parsing.mojo, http/request.mojo,
        http/response.mojo, server.mojo, strings.mojo, __init__.mojo,
        loop/request.mojo (2026-10-08): dead inherited code removed, each name searched for
        across the repository, the `m0` templates and the one application
        outside it, then the fork compiled whole: `StatusCode` and
        `HTTPResponse.is_redirect`, `content_length`, `connection_close`
        and `__len__` (and `Sized`); `RequestMethod`,
