      - header.mojo (2026-10-08): `find_header_end` backs off three bytes
        from `search_start` and never before the buffer's first byte; it
        started AT 1 to 3, so a CRLFCRLF opening the buffer was missed by a
        split read (review record LF66, SPEC B29; A3's LF59, the same
        defect and fix, is its duplicate).

      - http/parsing.mojo (2026-10-08): `http_parse_request_headers`
        refuses a CR that no LF follows before the request line (RFC 9112
        §2.2) rather than skipping it with the empty lines and reading the
        method from the byte after it, which `is_complete` refused when a
        read had ended after the CR (review record LF67, SPEC B29).

      - loop/state.mojo, websocket.mojo (2026-10-08): `_close_slot` replaces
        a provision's `recv_buffer` or `encoding_buffer` whose capacity is
        past `SLOT_BUFFER_KEEP` (64 KiB) with one of `buffer_size`;
        `WSState.feed` moves an assembled fragmented message out (`^`)
        where it copied it and cleared the original, and `WSState.reset`
        replaces both buffers where it cleared them (SPEC C17, I42).
        Cleared, an HTTP buffer kept the capacity of the largest request or
        response its slot had held, pinned to that slot for the life of the
        process, and a WebSocket buffer the capacity of its slot's last
        fragmented message or partial frame until the next replaced it
        (review record LF25). The threshold is `buffer_size` instead when
        that is larger, so a configured read size past 64 KiB is kept.

      - loop/request.mojo, server_config.mojo (2026-10-08): `_read_body`
        no longer compares the whole receive buffer with
        `recv_buffer_limit()`, and holds a chunked body to its decoded size
        and its raw cost after each decode, as the head path does, where it
        measured the decoded body plus the raw tail before decoding. The
        buffer holds the head and the next pipelined request beside the
        body: a `Content-Length` body with the next request in its last
        read was refused 400, a chunked body at the cap 413 when its
        framing or a request behind it came after the head, and a chunked
        body over the cap behind a long head 400 and closed rather than
        413 and lingered (review record LF70, SPEC C18). The head path's
        comparison of its whole buffer with `recv_buffer_limit()` stays,
        and is LF72's to change: it is what bounds a pipelined backlog.

