        use was `HTTPRequest.timeout`, a field the constructor wrote and
        nothing read, removed with its argument; and `prepare_loop`'s
        `debug_assert` that the listener's number is below `TIMER_HEADER`,
        which no shipped build compiles (a plain `debug_assert` runs only
        under `-D ASSERT=all`) and whose collision cannot happen: the
        listener has no timer, and a timer's event is dispatched by its
        filter (review record LF71).

      - loop/request.mojo, loop/state.mojo, loop/response.mojo,
        loop/timers.mojo, server.mojo, c/socket.mojo (2026-10-08): the
        pipelining drain reads nothing. `_drain_pipelined` framed each
        request behind an answer through `_handle_read_headers`, whose read
        came first, so a burst waiting in the socket was read whole in one
        pass, and the keep-alive reset copied the rest of the buffer after
        every answer: 20,000 pipelined GETs held the loop 1.56 s, the cost
        quadratic in the burst. A head is framed by `_frame_buffered`,
        which reads nothing; the read path takes one read and the drain
        answers what it brought. The reset advances
        `ConnectionProvision.head_start` past the answered request instead
        of copying, and the read path moves a partial head to the front
        once before its read (`compact_buffer`). A read that fills its
        buffer marks the slot's read interest spent (`_spend_read_edge`)
        and the drain's last step registers again, which on epoll is the
        only thing that announces the rest. The keep-alive reset keeps
        `peer_eof`, `_answers_the_last_request` peeks (`MSG_PEEK`) where it
        read, and an incomplete head alone is held to
        `recv_buffer_limit()`, where the whole buffer was compared (review
        record LF72, SPEC A41, C19).

      - loop/response.mojo, loop/request.mojo, loop/timers.mojo,
        server.mojo (2026-10-08): the interim `100 Continue` is sent by
        `_send_interim`, which keeps what a non-blocking send did not take
        in `ConnectionProvision.interim_owed`; `_finish_response` puts it
        ahead of the final response, and `_send_owed_interim` sends it
        ahead of the 400, 408 and 413 that can end the request. It was a
        best-effort `_send_raw_to_fd` (removed) that ignored the count, and
        on Linux a 25-byte send to a full socket can come back short
        (review record LF73, SPEC A42).

